ScanSafe.AI — Product Requirements Document
Platform: Android | Status: Proposed specification
1. Overview
ScanSafe.AI helps users scan food and beauty ingredient labels, review extracted text, and understand evidence-backed ingredient findings. It separates hazard evidence, allergens, regulatory restrictions, personal preferences, and unknown ingredients.

The product must not promise universal product safety, medical diagnosis, or counterfeit detection unless separately implemented and validated.
2. Goals
- Reduce the effort needed to read ingredient labels.
- Present plain-language findings with traceable sources.
- Allow correction of OCR errors.
- Support optional user preferences.
- Provide scan history and saved products with user controls.
- Support consumer subscriptions and possible B2B services.
3. Target users
1. Skincare and beauty consumers.
2. Food-label readers and families.
3. Users with dietary or ingredient preferences.
4. Later: retailers, publishers, and businesses needing ingredient analysis.
Recommended initial focus: skincare and beauty labels, expanding after coverage and accuracy are validated.
4. Core user journey
Launch → onboarding → home → scan or manual entry → capture/import image → OCR → review/correct text → analyze → view findings and evidence → save/share/report issue.
5. Functional requirements
- Onboarding: explain purpose and limitations; allow skip.
- Scanning: camera, image import, crop, multiple images, manual-entry fallback.
- OCR: preserve original extraction; allow correction; expose uncertainty.
- Ingredient matching: normalize names and aliases; keep unmatched items visible.
- Analysis: retrieve approved evidence; apply deterministic rules; use constrained AI only for supported explanations.
- Results: show findings, source links, jurisdiction/date when relevant, limitations, matched and unmatched ingredients.
- History: list, search, save, delete and export scans according to policy.
- Preferences: support optional dietary and ingredient preferences; keep preference mismatches distinct from hazard findings.
- Accounts: secure sign-in, recovery, sign-out and account deletion; guest mode is a product decision.
- Billing: configurable free quota, premium entitlements, purchase verification and restore.
- Feedback: report OCR or analysis issues for review.
6. Non-functional requirements
- Security: authentication, authorization, rate limits, secret management.
- Privacy: data minimization, consent, retention and deletion.
- Reliability: retryable requests, idempotency, monitoring.
- Accessibility: screen-reader support, text scaling, contrast, touch targets.
- Auditability: save evidence/rule versions used by each report.
- Maintainability: modular architecture, migrations, tests and CI/CD.
7. Metrics
First-scan completion, OCR correction rate, ingredient matching precision/recall, evidence coverage, unsupported-claim rate, 7/30-day retention, paid conversion, churn, cost per scan and feedback rate.
8. Acceptance criteria
- Users can scan, correct, and analyze a label.
- Substantive findings link to reviewed evidence.
- Unknown items are not automatically classified as harmful or harmless.
- Failed OCR or evidence retrieval is shown as a limitation/error.
- Users can inspect sources and delete retained data.
- Entitlements are verified server-side.
- Core flows pass accessibility, security, and device tests.     ScanSafe.AI — Design System and UI/UX Specification
Visual direction: Minimalist, editorial, hand-drawn botanical.
1. Principles
- Clarity before decoration.
- Evidence before claims.
- Calm, not alarmist.
- Show uncertainty rather than guessing.
- Color is never the only status indicator.
- Explain why permissions and data are needed.
